DATABASE_URL=postgres://remii:remii@localhost:5432/remii
REMII_DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10
# Credential vault encryption key. The example value is public and only suitable for local
# development. Production refuses to start with this key.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
KEY_ENCRYPTION_KEY=AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA=
# How many days of audit trail to keep. Unset keeps everything, which is the safe default and the
# one to leave alone until somebody has decided otherwise: the trail is append-only and nothing else
# can remove a row, so this is the only way it ever shrinks.
# AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=365
# Two names for one number, and they must agree when both are set.
#
# The server accepts either PORT or SERVER_PORT (server/src/config.ts), preferring PORT when both
# are present and refusing to start if they disagree, so a single edit is enough. An empty value
# is an unset one, so `PORT=` with SERVER_PORT set moves the server too. scripts/start.sh
# and the app's Vite proxy read SERVER_PORT/APP_PORT, and docs/configuration.md documents
# SERVER_PORT as the setting. Only PORT shipped here historically, so moving the server by
# editing one line left the script still looking at 3001: it found whatever else was there,
# accepted the first 200 as proof, and failed several stages later parsing that stranger's
# HTML as JSON. Setting both to the same value remains valid.
PORT=3001
SERVER_PORT=3001
TENANT_PACKAGE_DIR=../examples/consumer
# Let a Bot answer with an interface it wrote itself: markup, styles and a script it generates for
# that one answer, streamed into the transcript and rendered in a sandboxed iframe with no
# same-origin access to this app. On by default; set this to `false` or `0` to opt out.
#
# This is not the component catalogue. A component is something this deployment holds and a person
# grants per Bot; this has nothing to grant, because the Bot writes it on the spot and
# it is gone when the conversation moves on. It is a deployment switch rather than a per-Bot grant
# because the SDK offers no seam for one — see DeploymentConfig.generativeUi in server/src/config.ts.
#
# What the interface can reach is what the sandbox hands it, and this deployment hands it nothing: no
# session, no same-origin access, and no route into your data. It can load libraries from a CDN, so a
# deployment that must not reach the public internet from a browser tab should set this to false.
# REMII_GENERATIVE_UI=false
# What this deployment calls itself, when more than one shares a database. A copy of a
# deployment made for development uses the same database, and threads are listed per Bot with
# nothing to say which deployment a conversation came from. The name goes into every thread id this
# deployment mints, so its own conversations stay identifiable. Unset, the tenant package's id is
# used, which tells two packages apart but not two copies of one.
# DEPLOYMENT_ID=
# Sign-in. The line below runs the deployment as one local user with no sign-in at all, which is
# how you reach the product without registering an OAuth client first. Delete it and configure a
# provider before anybody else can reach this: while it is set, every visitor gets an account of
# their own. With no provider and this line gone, the deployment refuses to start rather than
# guessing.
REMII_SINGLE_USER=true
#
# SIGN-IN IS NEON AUTH, and NEON_AUTH_BASE_URL is the whole of it. Uncomment it once `neon deploy`
# has written the line for you.
# NEON_AUTH_BASE_URL=https://ep-xxxxxxx.neonauth.<region>.aws.neon.tech/neondb/auth
#
# The rest of this section.
#
# Sign-in is a hosted identity provider at an address, not a library configured inside this process.
# It holds the OAuth clients, the session secret and the trusted-origin list on its own side, per
# branch, so there is no client secret to write here and nothing to keep in step with it.
#
# Get the address from the CLI, which also writes it into .env:
#
#   neon link --project-id <your-project-id> --branch production
#   neon deploy
#
# `neon deploy` needs `auth: true` in neon.ts at the repository root. It provisions the provider on
# the linked branch and pulls DATABASE_URL and NEON_AUTH_* into .env.
#
# https only. The provider's session cookie is `__Secure-` prefixed and a browser refuses a `__Secure-`
# cookie that did not arrive over TLS, so an http:// address produces a sign-in that appears to work
# and leaves no session.
#
# Turn on the sign-in screen's email-and-password form. Whether the provider would ACCEPT one is its
# own setting, not this one: the server reads `neon_auth.project_config` at start-up and the screen
# draws a form only if the branch has password sign-in enabled.
# AUTH_EMAIL_PASSWORD=true
#
# The buttons the sign-in screen draws. Normally left alone — the server reads the branch's own
# provider configuration at start-up, so the buttons always match what this branch accepts.
#
# `none` draws NO social buttons at all, and that is worth knowing about rather than discovering.
#
# A social sign-in through this deployment's proxy does not finish. Google redirects to the identity
# provider's host, the provider sets its session cookie there, and this origin never sees it — so a
# Google button sends somebody there and returns them to the same screen, which reads as a rejected
# account rather than as an unfinished feature. Email and password sign-in works and is unaffected.
#
# So a deployment that has not settled the social flow should ship this, together with
# AUTH_EMAIL_PASSWORD=true, rather than offering a door that leads back to the sign-in screen. See
# docs/deployment.md for what would have to change to turn it back on.
#
# Any other value is a comma-separated list the provider admits: google, github, vercel. Anything else
# is dropped.
# NEON_AUTH_PROVIDERS=none

# Where this deployment is reached from outside. Also sent as the Origin on every call to the
# identity provider, which checks it against the branch's trusted-origin list — so a value here that
# the branch has not been told about will be refused. Needed for connectors that a person connects
# their own account to, such as Google Drive: it builds the redirect URI the vendor sends them back
# to, which has to match what you registered character for character.
# REMII_PUBLIC_URL=https://remii.example.com

# Where the browser app is served from. Defaults to the first TRUSTED_ORIGINS entry, then to
# REMII_PUBLIC_URL, which is right for a deployment serving everything from one origin.
# REMII_APP_URL=http://localhost:3010
TRUSTED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3010

# Threads, messages, runs and locks live in this deployment's own
# PostgreSQL (see server/src/threads/local): no cloud service to configure,
# no key to fetch, no licence. Older env files may still carry
# INTELLIGENCE_* variables; they are ignored.

# How long a Bot's stream may say nothing before this deployment gives up on the turn, in
# milliseconds. A Bot is any AG-UI endpoint, which means it will be redeployed mid-answer, its own
# upstream will time out, and it will sometimes accept a connection and then write nothing at all.
# Without this the channel stays busy, the composer stays locked, and the only way out is a reload.
#
# Silence, not duration. A turn may legitimately run for an hour while events keep arriving; what is
# measured here is the gap between them. Nothing about how long a Bot is allowed to work changes.
#
# A minute, because this deadline is in a race it has to win. Every Bot in this repository serves on
# Bun with `idleTimeout: 120`, and Bun tears a wedged streaming response down at roughly a second
# past that. A watchdog set to the same two minutes lands within a second of the socket dying, and
# whichever gets there first decides what the person sees: this deployment's sentence naming the Bot
# and saying the turn was ended, or "The socket connection was closed unexpectedly" and no audit row
# at all. Half the Bot's own idle timeout is far enough clear that the answer is always the first.
#
# A minute is still far longer than any real silence inside a run. The longest legitimate one is the
# wait for a model's first token, which is seconds. Browser tool calls do not need allowing for:
# they run between turns, not during one, because the run ends before the browser executes the tool
# and a second run carries the result back.
#
# 0, or leaving this unset, switches the watchdog off. Nothing is watched and no turn is ever ended.
AGENT_STALL_TIMEOUT_MS=60000

# How deep a chain of delegation may go. The number is the DEPTH, not the number of Bots: 2 is a
# chain of three, so an orchestrator hands to a specialist who hands to whoever delivers the result.
# The default is 2 for exactly that reason — at 1 the second hop was refused as "already 1 Bot deep"
# and any gather-then-deliver workflow simply could not run.
#
# 0 switches delegation off entirely. This is also the bound that stops a cycle (A asks B asks C asks
# A), so raise it only as far as a real workflow needs. Every hop is separately granted and audited,
# and BOT_HANDOFF_MAX_PER_RUN caps how many Bots one run may address.
BOT_HANDOFF_MAX_DEPTH=2
BOT_HANDOFF_MAX_PER_RUN=3

# Model key. Required by the proof-of-concept Bot, which speaks OpenAI's API directly, and by the
# framework Bot unless you point it at another provider below.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Key used ONLY for embeddings, so memory can rank a remembered sentence against a new question.
#
# Memory works without this — nothing here is required, and every read and write still succeeds — but
# what you get is a deterministic local word HASH rather than a trained model, and the difference is not
# cosmetic. The hash matches shared WORDS, not meaning.
#
# Measured, on queries of the shape the recall gate actually produces ("manager name", "employer
# organisation") against single stored facts: 3 of 6 recalled without a key here, and the failures are not
# near-misses. A query sharing no words at all with what is stored scores zero against a hash and cannot
# be recalled at any threshold — there is no similarity left to rank. So half of recall is unavailable
# without this key, and the honest summary is: dedup and near-duplicate detection work fine, automatic
# saving works fine, and cross-vocabulary recall does not.
#
# Set it to an OpenAI-compatible embeddings key for real recall. EMBEDDINGS_BASE_URL points it at a
# gateway serving the same `/v1/embeddings` API, the way OPENAI_BASE_URL does for chat. The server logs one
# warning at first use saying which of the two it is running, and names the similarity floor in force.
# EMBEDDINGS_API_KEY=
# EMBEDDINGS_BASE_URL=

# Whether the nightly memory consolidation actually writes.
#
# DRY RUN BY DEFAULT, and that default is the safe one: consolidation merges duplicate memories, resolves
# contradictions between them, and sweeps the ones nothing has recalled in months — all of which are
# destructive, and all of which run unattended on a schedule nobody is watching. With it unset the
# decisions are computed and logged and nothing is linked, so you can read what it would have done.
#
# Only the worker runs this, and only when the worker is running. `bun run dev` starts the app and the
# server, not the worker — so consolidation does not run in local development at all unless you start it.
MEMORY_CONSOLIDATE_DRY_RUN=true

# Where that key is spent. Unset, it is OpenAI. Set, it is any endpoint speaking the same
# `/v1/chat/completions` API: a gateway in front of several providers, a proxy, or a model on
# hardware you control. The API server reads it for the built-in agents and both shipped Bots read
# it too, so one line moves the whole deployment rather than one Bot.
#
# Model names travel verbatim, so use whatever the endpoint publishes. An endpoint that namespaces
# its catalogue wants both halves of the name, in `BOT_MODEL` and in the tenant package's
# `default_model` alike:
#
#   OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://gateway.internal/v1
#   OPENAI_API_KEY=...
#   BOT_MODEL=openai/gpt-5.5
#
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=

# Optional. Leave empty unless containers need a different route to the same compatible endpoint,
# such as a locally hosted model whose host URL is localhost but whose Compose-network URL is a
# service name. Empty means containers use OPENAI_BASE_URL too.
# OPENAI_CONTAINER_BASE_URL=

# The same for the other two providers, under the names the API server already reads. They are
# different APIs rather than different URLs for this one, so each has its own.
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=
# GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_BASE_URL=

# Framework Bot provider: openai, anthropic or google. It reads that provider's own
# key and refuses to start without it, so a deployment on Anthropic never needs an OpenAI key for it.
# The proof-of-concept Bot is OpenAI only by construction: it speaks that API directly.
# BOT_PROVIDER=openai
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_API_KEY=

# Which model the framework Bot uses. Defaults per provider: gpt-5.5, claude-sonnet-4-5,
# gemini-2.5-flash. A 5.6 tier works here: set one and the Responses API is switched on
# automatically. It used to answer nothing at all on those models — RUN_STARTED, RUN_FINISHED, no
# text — because that API streams content blocks rather than a string and the run read only the
# string. The default is left at 5.5 so the two shipped Bots stay comparable out of the box.
# BOT_MODEL=gpt-5.5

# OpenAI only, and rarely needed: the framework Bot turns the Responses API on by itself for models
# that require it. Set it when you are using a model this build has not heard of that needs it too.
# BOT_RESPONSES_API=false

# How hard the framework Bot thinks, on models that reason. OpenAI's setting, and one the Responses
# API carries, so it needs a model running on that API — a 5.6 tier does by itself, anything else
# wants BOT_RESPONSES_API=true. Unset, the model keeps its provider's own default.
#
# One of: none, minimal, low, medium, high, xhigh, max. Anything else and the Bot refuses to start
# and says so, rather than starting with a setting that quietly went nowhere.
# BOT_REASONING_EFFORT=

# THE COMPUTER: one desktop per PERSON, on E2B.
#
# There is no local computer any more. It was a Chromium in a container, one per Bot; the desktop now
# lives inside the person's own E2B sandbox, is created the first time somebody needs it, and persists
# until they delete it. Everything happens in there — the browser, the shell, the files. Every Bot of
# that person drives the same screen, so a computer per Bot would make the price depend on how many
# coworkers somebody created.
#
# THE KEY IS SERVER-SIDE AND NEVER REACHES REACT. A browser cannot reach a machine even by wanting to:
# it asks this server for the screen and is handed a noVNC URL plus a per-session password for that one
# desktop, minted when the stream starts and dead when it stops.
#
# Set E2B_API_KEY and there is a computer. Unset it and there is none at all, rather than one that
# exists and cannot be reached.
E2B_API_KEY=
# The API root. E2B's own default is correct for the hosted product; this is only for a self-hosted
# control plane.
# E2B_API_URL=https://api.e2b.dev
# The TEMPLATE the desktop is built from. `desktop` is E2B's own, and it is the only thing that makes a
# screen exist: Xvfb, XFCE, x11vnc, noVNC, xdotool, scrot, ffmpeg, Google Chrome and Python all come
# with it, so nothing has to be added.
#
# Worth knowing: `GET /templates` answers [] on an account with no CUSTOM templates, which reads like
# "there is no desktop available" and is wrong. The built-in is not listed by that endpoint.
# E2B_TEMPLATE=desktop
# How many of one person's desktops may run at once. 1 is the whole model; the cost is per sandbox per
# hour, and a person with many Bots should be on one screen rather than many.
# E2B_MAX_DESKTOPS_PER_USER=1
# Idle minutes before a desktop is PAUSED. 0 keeps it on always, which bills continuously for anyone
# who ever opened a computer.
#
# A pause, not a stop and not a delete. E2B's memory pause restores the desktop with its windows, its
# browser session and its running programs, and returns in seconds — so ten minutes is safe where a
# cold-boot stop had to be short. A paused sandbox is not billed at all.
E2B_AUTOSTOP_MINUTES=10
# Whether each person's files live on their own E2B VOLUME. On by default, and this is the persistence
# question in one boolean: a volume survives the sandbox being paused, killed or replaced, and sandbox
# disk does not survive the sandbox being deleted.
#
# One volume per person, deliberately. The volume name is an opaque hash of the user id, so it leaks
# nothing into an operator's volume list and a lost database row still finds its disk. `false` is the
# only value that turns this off — any other, a typo included, leaves it on.
E2B_VOLUMES=true
# Where the volume appears inside the sandbox. This is also what a tool's relative `notes.md` resolves
# against, so the two must agree; it lives in one place in the code for that reason.
# E2B_WORKSPACE_MOUNT=/workspace
# E2B's own kill-clock, and it is a HARD ceiling: a sandbox reaching `timeoutMs` is DELETED, taking the
# desktop's windows and running programs with it. One hour on Hobby, 24 on Pro. The server pushes this
# forward while a computer is in use, so it is a backstop rather than the thing that kills a desktop —
# leave it unset unless you know your account's cap.
# E2B_SANDBOX_TIMEOUT_MS=
# The desktop's resolution, set when the sandbox is created. The agent and the viewer read the real
# geometry back from the running desktop, so nothing depends on this being right.
# VNC_RESOLUTION_WIDTH=1920
# VNC_RESOLUTION_HEIGHT=1080
# The secret every call to a desktop must present. Reaches only the server.
COMPUTER_TOKEN=


# Per-Bot egress identity. A proxy the Bot's browser sends everything through, so the far side sees a
# stable address it can allow-list or attribute to that Bot. `EGRESS_PROXY_<BOT>` names one Bot,
# `EGRESS_PROXY_DEFAULT` covers the rest, and absent means the browser goes out directly.
#
# Credentials may be in the URL and are split out before Playwright sees them. Only the HOST is ever
# reported back on any screen or the API, so the password does not end up in front of anybody.
#
# This is attribution, not anonymity, and it is not a boundary by itself: it gives a security team a
# per-Bot address for network rules alongside AGENT_COMPUTER_POLICY.
#
# These go in `egress.env` beside this file, NOT here. The names are per-Bot, so Compose cannot
# list them the way it lists every variable below, and it hands a container only what it is told to.
# In `.env` they reach no process and the browser goes out directly with nothing saying so.
#
#   EGRESS_PROXY_DEFAULT=http://user:password@proxy.internal:8080
#   EGRESS_PROXY_SALES_BOT=http://sales.proxy.internal:8080


# The managed coworker AG-UI endpoint. Optional: use an HTTP(S) URL, and set MANAGED_AGENT_TOKEN
# with it. A URL with no token refuses to start; a leftover token with no URL is ignored.
#
# Unset in the one-container image, which does not carry a Bot. `scripts/start.sh` points this at
# agent-langgraph on 4201 for a laptop. The proof-of-concept on 4200 hand-writes the protocol and
# leaves the loop to whatever is watching, so it is a reference rather than something to build a
# deployment on.
# MANAGED_AGENT_AG_UI_URL=http://localhost:4201/ag-ui

# Shared secret sent by the server on every call to a managed Bot, as the `x-remii-agent-token`
# header. Required together with the URL above; the server starts without either.
#
# `scripts/start.sh` generates one and writes it back here on first run, so leaving this empty is
# fine locally. Set it yourself for a deployment: openssl rand -base64 32
MANAGED_AGENT_TOKEN=

# The second Bot in the box runs on http://localhost:4201/ag-ui, on a framework rather than
# proof of concept, and is reached the same way: point MANAGED_AGENT_AG_UI_URL at it, or add it as a
# Bot of its own in the tenant package or at /agents.

# Which model the Bots use. BOT_MODEL is the framework Bot's: it runs gpt-5.6-terra and switches to
# the Responses API by itself, because 5.6 rejects function tools on /v1/chat/completions.
#
# The proof-of-concept Bot has its own, AGENT_BOT_MODEL, defaulting to gpt-5.5, because it writes
# that endpoint by hand and refuses to start on a model whose tools it cannot use. One variable for
# both would mean setting the framework Bot's model quietly took the other one's tools away.
# AGENT_BOT_MODEL=gpt-5.5
# BOT_RESPONSES_API=false

# Set to runsc to run every computer under gVisor, if the host has it. Unset, a computer is an
# ordinary container and shares the host kernel, which is worth knowing when the Bot is not ours.
COMPUTER_RUNTIME=

# The secret a framework Bot presents when it calls a tool back through this server. A Bot runs its
# own loop, in its own process, and still may not reach a vendor directly: it calls the deployment
# that granted the tool, which is where the grant, the policy and the audit row are. Absent, no Bot
# may call tools back and it is told so rather than being quietly allowed.
AGENT_TOOL_TOKEN=

# The secret the routine worker presents when it hands a run back to this server. It authenticates
# the handoff of one routine run id, and nothing else: the server re-reads the routine, the Bot and
# everything else it needs from the database, so the worker cannot use this to inject an instruction.
#
# Left empty, the internal endpoint refuses every call and no routine ever fires — the correct state
# for a deployment that has not stood up a worker. Set for one that has: openssl rand -base64 32.
# Do not accept a default in production.
WORKER_SHARED_SECRET=

# Composio, the broker that holds people's accounts for a few hundred apps, so a Bot can act in Gmail
# or Slack or Notion without this deployment registering an OAuth client with each of them.
#
# Optional. Without this key there is nothing to connect, nothing to grant, and no Composio tool for
# a Bot to call, and the whole Composio section of Settings -> App connections is not drawn at all.
# There is no directory to browse, no picker, and no brokered app on anybody's connected-accounts page.
#
# This variable is the whole of the setting. There is no screen anywhere in the product that sets it:
# it is read from this environment at startup, and nobody can turn Composio on from a page.
#
# One key for the whole deployment, not one per person. Composio keeps people apart by a user id sent
# with every call, so one account's connections are never reachable from another's.
COMPOSIO_API_KEY=

# GitHub, for the repository a skill may point at. Only used to read PUBLIC repositories, and a skill
# never carries a credential: this token is the deployment's, spent by the server, and nothing a person
# writes on the Skills page can reach it.
#
# Optional, and unset is the ordinary case. GitHub allows an unauthenticated caller 60 requests an
# hour, and a deployment shares that across everybody using it, so a busy one will eventually meet the
# limit. Setting this raises it to 5,000 an hour. It does NOT enable private repositories: those are
# refused either way, because a private repository would stop being public content and a skill that
# adds no capability could then add one.
#
# Either a fine-grained token with public repository read access, or a classic token with `public_repo`.
# Leave empty in a deployment nobody has pointed a skill at a repository on.
GITHUB_API_TOKEN=

# How Bots act by default: `direct` (do what was asked, immediately, with the tools held) or
# `ask-first` (external, side-effecting actions wait for the person's word via ask_person
# first). Internal work is never gated either way. Unset means `direct`. A person overrides
# this for themselves on the General settings screen, which is what most deployments want;
# set this only to change what they inherit.
# BOT_EXECUTION_MODE=direct

# Live web search for Bots (Remi web_search/web_open tools). Unset, the tools are not offered
# and the browser remains the fallback. Point at any freeapi-compatible search endpoint.
# WEB_SEARCH_API=
# WEB_SEARCH_URL=https://web.freeapi.space

# Voice and photo understanding on Telegram (transcription, image description). Unset, voice
# notes and photos arrive as captions with a note that the media went unread; text keeps working.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Spoken Telegram replies through ElevenLabs (voice notes earn voice answers). Unset, everything
# stays text. Voice id picks the voice; model picks the multilingual engine.
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=
# ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID=
# ELEVENLABS_MODEL=eleven_multilingual_v2

# Google Workspace through this machine's `gog` CLI: no hosted OAuth, the
# machine holds its own credentials. Unset, `gog` is resolved off PATH. Authenticate once with
# `gog auth add you@example.com --services gmail,drive,calendar,tasks --readonly` and the
# gog_* tools switch on by themselves.
# GOG_BINARY=/home/remii/go/bin/gog


# The model. This deployment is DeepSeek-only: one provider, one model (deepseek-flash), no
# fallback chain. REMI_FLASH_MODEL overrides the model name for a Pro-tier key.
# With no key the loop refuses with one sentence naming this setting instead of calling out.
# DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=
# REMI_FLASH_MODEL=deepseek-flash
# The abliterated model ("Darkside" in Settings). OpenAI-compatible at api.abliteration.ai/v1.
# ABLITERATION_API_KEY=

# Shared secret Composio trigger webhooks must carry (x-composio-secret header). Unset, events
# are accepted when they resolve to a local user; set it in production so only Composio knocks.
# COMPOSIO_TRIGGER_SECRET=
# Where Composio sends app events: POST this URL as the trigger callback in Composio's dashboard.
# https://remii.example.com/api/webhooks/composio

# Billing (Dodo Payments). One plan ($49/mo Pro) plus a $7/mo BYOK add-on;
# new accounts get a 5-hour free trial from signup.
#
# DODO_PAYMENTS_API_KEY=            # server key from the Dodo dashboard
# DODO_PAYMENTS_WEBHOOK_SECRET=     # signs /api/webhooks/dodo; unsigned webhooks are refused in production
# DODO_PAYMENTS_ENVIRONMENT=test_mode  # or live_mode
# DODO_PRODUCT_ID_PRO=              # $49/mo Pro subscription product
# DODO_PRODUCT_ID_BYOK=             # $7/mo BYOK add-on product
# DODO_PRODUCT_ID_TOPUP=            # one-time credit top-up product (packs pass credits in metadata)
